<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Jena</h1><p class="st-meta">1672 CE – 1690 CE · capital Jena</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Brief Ernestine duchy centred on the university town of Jena, carved out for a younger Weimar prince and gone within two decades.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-duke-of-saxe-weimar">William of Saxe-Weimar</a> died in 1662 and his lands were divided among his sons. In 1672, after a further rearrangement following the extinction of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>, Bernhard received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and its surroundings as the Duchy of Saxe-Jena.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bernhard ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, rebuilding the castle there and maintaining a modest Lutheran court. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a>, founded in 1558, was shared among the Ernestine dukes and remained the town's main institution. The duchy was too small to pursue an independent policy and relied on its larger Ernestine relatives.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bernhard died in 1678 and his only son John William died as a child in 1690. The duchy was then divided between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-eisenach">Duchy of Saxe-Eisenach</a> and Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> itself eventually going to Weimar.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 1672 CE – 1690 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.98%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1672 CE – 1690 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jena</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bernhard, John William</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sachsen-Jena</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1681&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-jena" aria-label="Duchy of Saxe-Jena on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4943 411 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Saxe-Jena lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5263 667L5257 666M5263 667L5235 666"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5257 666h0M5235 666h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5263 667h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5263 667h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jena. <a href="/atlas?year=1681&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-jena">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
